<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edict of Expulsion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1290">1290</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Edict of Expulsion was a royal decree issued by Edward I on 18 July 1290 that expelled all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, the first time a European state is known to have permanently banned their presence. It followed punitive taxation, the Statute of the Jewry of 1275, which outlawed lending at interest, and the death sentences on over 300 Jews in the coin-clipping crisis of the late 1270s. The Jews, perhaps as few as 2,000, could take cash and personal possessions, but their debts, homes, synagogues and cemeteries were forfeited to the king, and some died through piracy or drowned in the Channel. It stood until 1656, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> informally permitted their resettlement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edict-of-expulsion.jpg" alt="Edict of Expulsion" width="330" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BritLibCottonNeroDiiFol183vPersecutedJews.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Jewish communities were first recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> some time after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> of 1066, and their members were treated as the king's own property, to be taxed or imprisoned at his will. Church restrictions, the badge decreed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-council-of-the-lateran">Fourth Lateran Council</a> in 1215 and adopted at the Synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> in 1222, and accusations of ritual murder such as the case of Little Saint Hugh of Lincoln deepened hostility. Heavy royal taxation forced Jewish lenders to sell their bonds cheaply to courtiers, who seized indebted knights' lands; Simon de Montfort turned the resulting anger into violence in which 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><p>In 1287, recovering from a fall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, Edward I expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of that duchy, and in 1289 his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-naples">Charles of Salerno</a> expelled those of Maine and Anjou. By his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1289 Edward was deeply in debt and his attempt to convert the Jews had failed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 14 June 1290 Edward summoned the knights of the shires, the group most hostile to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and usury, to Parliament, and on 18 June he secretly ordered the chests recording Jewish debts sealed. Parliament met on 15 July and granted a tax of £116,000, probably the highest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>; whether the expulsion was offered for the vote or demanded in return is argued both ways. The edict, whose text is lost, fell on 9 Av, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisha-bav">Tisha B'Av</a>, and writs ordered all Jews gone by 1 November.</p><p>Proclamations forbade harming those leaving, and the Cinque Ports were told to arrange safe passage and cheap fares for the poor; about 1,300 poor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> crossed to Wissant near Calais for 4d each. On 10 October a captain sailing poor London Jews to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> left them on a sandbank near Queenborough as the tide rose. Many refugees probably settled in France; others reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The seized properties raised about £2,000, and 85 grants went to courtiers, the Church and the royal circle. On 5 November Edward told the Barons of the Exchequer that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> had been expelled in honour of the Crucified, and he promoted the cult of Little Saint Hugh. Contemporaries counted the expulsion among his achievements, and it is widely seen as a precedent for the Alhambra Decree of 1492. It embedded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a> in English culture. Jews achieved formal equality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> by 1858, and in May 2022 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> held an act of repentance on the 800th anniversary of the Synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edict of Expulsion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Edward I's <b>Edict of Expulsion</b> had banned them in 1290.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-18">18 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Edward I issued the <b>Edict of Expulsion</b>, ordering all Jews to leave England by 1 November.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-18">18 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
